sysl.regex — what a pattern parses to
=====================================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
    import sysl.text.Ascii

A regular expression is a tree, and this file is the tree plus the two things hanging off it: the
set a bracket expression stands for, and the ways a pattern can refuse to parse.

The engine matches **characters**, not bytes. A `string` in sysl is UTF-8 by construction (`04`),
so a `.` standing for one byte would be a promise the type does not make — it would match half of a
two-byte character and hand back a span that `s[a..<b]` then refuses to cut. Every position the
engine reports is a **byte offset at a character boundary**, which is exactly what a slice takes.


The tree
--------

The AST is a recursive enum, reaching itself through `&Node`. That edge is what makes the cycle
finite (`03 § Recursive types`), and it is the whole reason this reads as a tree at all: the same
engine in the older sysl had no recursive types, so its AST was a flat array of five-field structs
addressed by integer index, with `-1` standing for "no child" and bracket expressions kept in a
side array reached by a second index. Every walk over it was an index lookup the type system could
not check, and a node holding a child index into the wrong array was a wrong answer rather than a
compile error.

`Empty` is a node rather than an absence for the same reason. `a|` is a legal pattern whose second
branch matches the empty string, and the older engine said so by returning `-1` from the branch
parser — which is also what it returned for a parse failure, so the two could not be told apart.

    enum Node
        Empty
        Lit(ch: char)
        AnyChar
        AtStart
        AtEnd
        InSet(of: CharClass)
        Seq(left: &Node, right: &Node)
        Alt(left: &Node, right: &Node)
        Star(inner: &Node)
        Plus(inner: &Node)
        Quest(inner: &Node)
        Group(inner: &Node, index: int)
    end Node

A pattern does not reach through a reference the way a field selection does, so every walk below
starts by taking the reference apart with `*n match`. The compiler says so precisely when that is
forgotten, which is how it was learned here rather than guessed.


The named classes
-----------------

`[[:alpha:]]` and its eleven siblings are the one part of a bracket expression that is not a range.
They are kept as what they are rather than expanded into ranges, so that each one is answered by
the classifier that already exists for it in `sysl.text.Ascii` — `[[:punct:]]` is `is_punct`, not
four hand-typed runs of the ASCII table that a reader has to check against it.

Each is ASCII-only, which is what POSIX means by them in the C locale, and a character above 127
answers `false` to every one. That is the trait's own guarantee rather than something restated here.

    enum NamedClass
        Alpha
        Digit
        Alnum
        Upper
        Lower
        Space
        Blank
        Print
        Graph
        Cntrl
        Punct
        XDigit
    end NamedClass

    private admits_named(n: NamedClass, c: char) -> bool = n match
        Alpha -> c.is_alpha()
        Digit -> c.is_digit()
        Alnum -> c.is_alnum()
        Upper -> c.is_upper()
        Lower -> c.is_lower()
        Space -> c.is_space()
        Blank -> c.is_blank()
        Print -> c.is_print()
        Graph -> c.is_graph()
        Cntrl -> c.is_control()
        Punct -> c.is_punct()
        XDigit -> c.is_hex_digit()
    end admits_named

    private named_spelling(n: NamedClass) -> string = n match
        Alpha -> "alpha"
        Digit -> "digit"
        Alnum -> "alnum"
        Upper -> "upper"
        Lower -> "lower"
        Space -> "space"
        Blank -> "blank"
        Print -> "print"
        Graph -> "graph"
        Cntrl -> "cntrl"
        Punct -> "punct"
        XDigit -> "xdigit"
    end named_spelling

    named_class(name: string) -> Option[NamedClass] = name match
        "alpha" -> Some(Alpha)
        "digit" -> Some(Digit)
        "alnum" -> Some(Alnum)
        "upper" -> Some(Upper)
        "lower" -> Some(Lower)
        "space" -> Some(Space)
        "blank" -> Some(Blank)
        "print" -> Some(Print)
        "graph" -> Some(Graph)
        "cntrl" -> Some(Cntrl)
        "punct" -> Some(Punct)
        "xdigit" -> Some(XDigit)
        else None
    end named_class


What a bracket expression stands for
------------------------------------

`[a-z0-9_[:space:]]` is a union of inclusive ranges and named classes, and `[^...]` is its
complement. Both are one shape: ask whether the character falls in any member, then flip the answer
if the class was negated.

Ranges are over `u32` code points rather than over `char` so that the comparison is ordinary
integer arithmetic on the value a character already carries.

    struct CharRange
        lo: u32
        hi: u32
    end CharRange

    struct CharClass
        ranges: &Buf[CharRange]
        names: &Buf[NamedClass]
        negated: bool

        // Whether the class admits this character. The members are walked rather than looked up: a
        // bracket expression holds a handful of them, and a bitmap would be both bigger and wrong
        // for the characters above ASCII that `[^a]` has to admit.
        admits(self, c: char) -> bool
            var hit = false
            var v = u32(c)

            for i in 0..<self.ranges.len()
                var r = self.ranges.at(i)

                if v >= r.lo && v <= r.hi
                    hit = true
                    break

            if !hit
                for i in 0..<self.names.len()
                    if admits_named(self.names.at(i), c)
                        hit = true
                        break

            if self.negated then !hit else hit
        end admits
    end CharClass

    char_class() -> CharClass = CharClass(buf(), buf(), false)


Why a pattern was refused
-------------------------

Every case carries the offset in the *pattern* where the trouble is, which is what a caller needs
to point at the character the author got wrong. The older engine carried a `string` message beside
a `bool` flag; an enum is matched exhaustively instead, so a caller that wants to treat one case
differently — an unknown class name is a typo, an unterminated group is a missing character — can,
and adding a case makes every such caller say what it does about it.

    enum RegexError
        TrailingBackslash(at: usize)
        UnterminatedClass(at: usize)
        UnterminatedClassName(at: usize)
        UnknownClassName(at: usize, name: string)
        UnterminatedGroup(at: usize)
        UnmatchedParen(at: usize)
        UnterminatedInterval(at: usize)
        MissingIntervalCount(at: usize)
        BackwardsInterval(at: usize)
        BackwardsRange(at: usize)
        NothingToRepeat(at: usize)
        PatternTooLarge
    end RegexError

`PatternTooLarge` is the one that carries no position, because it is not about a character: it is
the whole pattern being more than the compiler will lay out. Intervals stack multiplicatively —
`a{200}{200}{200}` is sixteen characters and eight million instructions — so a pattern arriving from
somewhere untrusted can exhaust memory at *compile* time however well the matching is bounded. The
limit is what keeps the engine's promise from having a hole in it.

    describe(e: RegexError) -> string = e match
        TrailingBackslash(at) -> s"a backslash ends the pattern, at $at"
        UnterminatedClass(at) -> s"a bracket expression is never closed, opened at $at"
        UnterminatedClassName(at) -> s"a [:name:] is never closed, opened at $at"
        UnknownClassName(at, name) -> s"there is no character class named '$name', at $at"
        UnterminatedGroup(at) -> s"a group is never closed, opened at $at"
        UnmatchedParen(at) -> s"a ')' closes a group that was never opened, at $at"
        UnterminatedInterval(at) -> s"an interval is never closed, opened at $at"
        MissingIntervalCount(at) -> s"an interval needs a count, at $at"
        BackwardsInterval(at) -> s"an interval counts down rather than up, at $at"
        BackwardsRange(at) -> s"a range runs from high to low, at $at"
        NothingToRepeat(at) -> s"a repetition has nothing before it to repeat, at $at"
        PatternTooLarge -> s"the pattern expands past $max_program instructions"
    end describe


Reading a tree back
-------------------

`show` renders a node as a parenthesised term, which is what the parser is checked against. It is
public rather than a test fixture because it is also the answer to "what did my pattern actually
parse to", and a caller debugging a bracket expression that admits one character too many wants to
see it — Go exposes its regex AST for the same reason.

The form is deliberately not the pattern it came from. Round-tripping to pattern text would hide
exactly the precedence mistakes the parser can make, because `a|bc` and `(a|b)c` differ only in
where the parentheses go and both are legal patterns.

    show(n: &Node) -> string = *n match
        Empty -> "empty"
        Lit(c) -> s"lit($c)"
        AnyChar -> "any"
        AtStart -> "start"
        AtEnd -> "end"
        InSet(cls) -> show_set(cls)
        Seq(l, r) -> s"seq(${show(l)}, ${show(r)})"
        Alt(l, r) -> s"alt(${show(l)}, ${show(r)})"
        Star(i) -> s"star(${show(i)})"
        Plus(i) -> s"plus(${show(i)})"
        Quest(i) -> s"quest(${show(i)})"
        Group(i, k) -> s"group$k(${show(i)})"
    end show

A range whose ends are equal came from a single character and is shown as one, so `[abc]` reads as
`set(a,b,c)` rather than as three ranges that happen to be degenerate.

    private show_set(cls: CharClass) -> string
        var out = if cls.negated then "notset(" else "set("
        var wrote = false

        for i in 0..<cls.ranges.len()
            var r = cls.ranges.at(i)

            if wrote then out += ","
            out += if r.lo == r.hi then str(char(r.lo)) else s"${char(r.lo)}-${char(r.hi)}"
            wrote = true

        for i in 0..<cls.names.len()
            if wrote then out += ","
            out += s"[:${named_spelling(cls.names.at(i))}:]"
            wrote = true

        out + ")"
    end show_set
